WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.984
Detection engineering increasingly adopts software engineering practices.

00:00:02.984 --> 00:00:07.103
Teams use version control, peer review, testing, documentation, change management,

00:00:07.103 --> 00:00:11.635
CI/CD workflows, and lifecycle processes to manage detection content more consistently.

00:00:11.635 --> 00:00:15.755
Detection-as-Code does not mean every organization needs the same toolchain.

00:00:15.755 --> 00:00:20.698
The core idea is that detections should be treated as controlled, reviewable,

00:00:20.698 --> 00:00:25.230
testable assets rather than informal edits made directly in a console.

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:31.821
Testing helps teams validate detection behavior before deployment and after changes to data sources or platforms.

00:00:31.821 --> 00:00:35.941
Documentation helps analysts and engineers understand purpose, expected behavior, dependencies,

00:00:35.941 --> 00:00:37.177
and response guidance.

00:00:37.177 --> 00:00:40.884
Engineering practices also make detection programs easier to scale.

00:00:40.884 --> 00:00:45.828
As the number of detections grows, teams need consistent naming, ownership, metadata,

00:00:45.828 --> 00:00:49.947
review schedules, and release processes to keep the program maintainable.

00:00:49.947 --> 00:00:52.007
This approach supports safer change.

00:00:52.007 --> 00:00:55.303
When detection logic is reviewed, tested, and documented,

00:00:55.303 --> 00:00:59.835
teams are less likely to introduce avoidable noise, break existing coverage,

00:00:59.835 --> 00:01:03.542
or lose track of why a detection was created.

00:01:03.542 --> 00:01:05.602
It also makes onboarding easier.

00:01:05.602 --> 00:01:10.958
New analysts and engineers can read the detection history, understand the intended behavior,

00:01:10.958 --> 00:01:15.489
and see how detections connect to telemetry sources and response workflows.

00:01:15.489 --> 00:01:17.549
Engineering discipline also supports accountability.

00:01:17.549 --> 00:01:21.669
Teams can define owners, service expectations, release steps, rollback options,

00:01:21.669 --> 00:01:28.260
and review cadences so detection content is managed with the same care as other production capabilities.
